A||N||23||Monkeyman||Kleenex, please--Computer died||||9||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||01-16-2001||06:08 PM||||Apparently I got a trojan horse type of virus the other day.  Now my computer won't boot up.  I called Gateway (bought the computer from them) and they said that the only cure would be to erase everything and reinstall Windows from scratch.<P>I had so much on the computer that I didn't save to a CD-ROM or a floppy disk.  I had well over 2500 pics that I had collected as well as some downloaded programs, email addys, bits and pieces of info, etc, etc.  The worst thing is that I had most of a novel saved that I've been working on for almost 10 years.  The rough draft was almost finished.  Now that's gone forever.  (Not that I'll be the next Stephen King, but I don't think it was bad.)  Thank God I have a backup laptop, but it's just not the same.  Gateway said that there's a slight chance that they can save everything by just deleting and reinstalling Windows (98--I'll lose ME, but I don't think that's a great loss) but I'm out of warranty.  They said that I've lost Explorer (whatever that is).  <P>Sorry.  I'm just rambling, trying not to cry.  My computer has gotten me through some rough times (not a physical attachment, but a link to everywhere and everything other than God).<P>If anyone thinks they can help restore at least some of my info, please email me @ dandc1@hoosierlink.com .  I know that some of you are computer geniuses, but I also realize that you lead busy lives.  I promise to upgrade McAfee/Norton next time and use them on a regular basis. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||9
Z||000001||Cliff Pennock||01-16-2001||06:38 PM||||Well, that's just a load of BS. There is no way they can diagnose a computer over the telephone let alone tell you that the only cure is to erase everything and reinstall Windows.<P>First of all, you need to get the computer booting. If the computer doesn't POST (POwer up Self Test) then erasing your harddisk won't do any good, besides, that would be a neat trick considering the computer doesn't even start.<P>If you get past POST (past the memory test, and past the harddisk detection) then there's a 100% chance you can boot your machine in some kind of operating system.<P>Also, WinMe has some provision to revert your operating system back to a situation where you <B>were</B> able to boot WinMe. I don't use WinMe ('cos I really, really, <B>really</B> hate it) so I'm not 100% sure how to do that, but I'm sure there are others here that can tell you exactly how. If that doesn't work, you can try the following:<P>Bbefore you do anything, we need to save (as in rescue) your data first.<P>First you need a boot-floppy, preferably from Win98. Just find yourself a Win98 machine, and open the <B>Control Panel</B>. Double click on the <B>Add/Remove Programs</B> icon and choose the "Start-up diskette" tab (I'm not sure what that's called in English, since we here use the Dutch version of Win). Follow the on-screen instructions.<P>Now put this start-up diskette in your own machine and boot it up. You will probably see a small text-menu pop-up with options like "start with CDROM support" etc. Choose any one.<P>After it has gone through the complete boot process, a dos-prompt (A:&gt; ) will appear. type <B>C:</B> and press enter. Next, type <B>dir</B> and press enter. If you see a lot of files flying by, then there's nothing wrong with your harddisk itself. Now there are several ways to salvage your files using floppy disks, but that could be a long and cumbersome process.<P>If you have a laptop with a USB port, and a (good) friend with a PC and a USB port too, there's an faster and easier way. What you do is remove the harddisk from your PC and install it as a second harddisk on the secondary port in your friend's PC. Windows will immediately detect this second harddisk and with a special USB cable and software (about $30) you can transfer all important files to your laptop.<P>There are many, many more ways to salvage your harddisk. Erasing everything and reinstall Windows <B>isn't</B> one of them. Even reinstalling Windows only can result in a loss of files.<P>Like I said earlier, I don't work with WinMe (anymore) so perhaps there are easier ways to get your data back. Anyone?||||reg||1
Z||000002||DaRkLoRD||01-16-2001||06:47 PM||||I agree 100% with Cliff.... I've had something similar happen. I was hit with the Chernobyl (CIH) virus back in 1999, and I thought I'd lost everything.. but I waited and a program was developed and it restored my partition tables so I was able to get everything back.. now I have a cd burner and I backed up about 110mb of my files last night.. I have many more to put on cd in the next few days.<P>anyway, don't delete your hard drive!! try what Cliff said and there's a good chance you can get the files back.<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||4
Z||000003||Oreif||01-16-2001||07:26 PM||||Go to F-prot's site. They have an anti-virus program that will fit on a floppy and it's a boot up disk. You just put the disk in and power up the computer. It will automatically seek out the virus and remove it. It will also repair what some things.  Gennerally the virus's attack the boot sector or the windows programs so your pics and personal stuff should be unaffected. Also if you know the name of the virus, F-prot's site has detailed info on exactly what it attacked and how to repair it. <BR>The site is<BR>http//www.f-secure.com <BR>Go under "Products" You can type in the virus name in the search.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||01-16-2001||07:59 PM||||Don't despair, MM. There is still hope for recovering most of the harddrive. Monkey, you gotta keep things backed up. Harddrives do wear out, you know? Not to mention viruses. Speaking of which...you had a virus once before. You HAVE to use an up to date antivirus program. Sorry if I'm sounding like Dad, but young man, you know better...LOL!<P>The techs don't care about saving your data. Viruses are sometimes hard to recover from even when you're in front of the computer. It's MUCH easier for them to have you reformat your harddrive and reinstall Windows. If I were doing it, I'd put that drive on my machine. When it boots, the virus will try to infect my harddrive and my Norton's will exterminate it, and hopefully, repair the damage it caused. Then I'd back everything up that was critical to CDs. Try the boot disk method like Cliff explained first. If you can get to DOS, then the files can be copied.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Cheever2||01-16-2001||08:08 PM||||Cliff, why do you hate me so much?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR>No, I mean, why do you hate Win<B>ME</B> so much?  Is it nitty-gritty technical things, that the average fat-dumb-n-happy idiots like me wouldn't notice anyway?  My new Compaq has it but I haven't yet found out what the difference is.<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000006||Cliff Pennock||01-16-2001||08:29 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cheever2:<BR><B>...but I haven't yet found out what the difference is.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That's one reason.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>There are several more reasons. I hate the fact that it's actually being shipped with Win<B>98</B> drivers that do not work under WinMe. I hate the fact they tell you it's a new product while it's not. I hate the fact they tell you it does not run on top of Dos anymore and that's the reason there's no way to open a Dos-box while it does and there is. I hate the fact WinMe crashes on most USB devices. And so on, and so on...<P>But let me clarify something here: the only contact I've had so far with WinMe is on other people's machines. And I usually work on other people's machines to solve some kind of problem they have with Windows. And my experience with troubleshooting WinMe is that it is even less intuitive than Win9x was.<P>Then again, I'm a die-hard MS-hater and my judgement is therefore very, <B>very</B> clouded on this subject.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I could be very, very wrong about WinMe and it could be the best thing since sliced bread (what was the best thing <B>before</B> sliced bread?) but I'd rather eat kitty-litter then admit to that.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Now CP/U - <B>that</B> was an operating system! (j/k of course, CP/U was baaaaaaad)||||reg||1
Z||000007||p4n1c||01-16-2001||08:56 PM||||CP/U or CP/M?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  And WinME is horrible, I'm running it right now, probably going back to 2000.  It crashes left right and center, is way less stable then 98.<P>Or just keep using BeOS or Linux.  Both are on here, it's just that I've been playing MW4.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>-=-=-=[ p4n1c ]=-=-=-<BR>"Each passing day, every passing face seems like such a blur" <BR>- The Offspring(Million Miles Away)||||reg||1
Z||000008||Monkeyman||01-16-2001||09:38 PM||||So, there's hope for me after all????  Outstanding!  I'll try some of those suggestions tomorrow after work.  It sure would be nice to save at least some of my data.  <P>Cliff--I don't know anyone with another PC, but if I have to, I'll just find a way to copy everything to a bunch of floppys.  It may take a while, but the other alternative (losing everything) is much worse.<P>Oreif--I don't know what the name of the virus is.  I'm not even 100% sure that's the problem although that seems to be the general consensus.  I'll check out that site tomorrow and see what they can do to help me.<P>Here's exactly what happens when I turn my PC on.  I get the usual WinME startup screen, then I get the background pic I've been using (pic of a monkey--surprising, I know).  Then I get a series of info windows with a close button in the middle.  The windows say something about EXE____ has caused an error in 03_ by 32.DLL then almost what looks like a long version of time (something like 04hrs37min65h--not exactly but similar).  If it would make a difference, I'll try to boot the PC again tomorrow and copy the exact message for you all to see.  Once I click on Close in all of the little windows (about a half dozen or so), I get the Win startup sound effects, then nothing.  The background stays and my cursor moves, but nothing happens.<P>You guys are great.  I'll get back here tomorrow with some updated info that might help diagnose the problem a little bit better.||||reg||1
Z||000009||DaRkLoRD||01-16-2001||10:16 PM||||Monkey, have you tried booting into safe mode? it bypasses most of the startup drivers, which could be causing problems with a normal system boot.<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||5
Z||000010||DaRkLoRD||01-16-2001||10:17 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Monkeyman:<BR><B>(Not that I'll be the next Stephen King, but I don't think it was bad.)</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>woohoo!! someone wants to be me!! LOL.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>well, sorta..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||10
Z||000011||batboy||01-17-2001||12:16 AM||||You might not have a virus. It could be that Windows is screwed up. Sounds like the registry got corrupted, which happened often enough with past versions of Windows. Monkey, you said it wouldn't boot up in your first post. It's boot up, it's just bombing off in the Windows startup. Go into Safe Mode. At least you'll be able to copy stuff. If you can get to Safe Mode, it's most likely recoverable.||||reg||1
Z||000012||GT Bastard||01-17-2001||12:16 AM||||Righto Darklord....  Monkey, when you hear your system beep, start tapping your F8 key until you see the startup menu.  Choose safe mode from the list using your arrow keys and hit enter.  It should load the desktop in a "generic" looking mode and you'll get a message saying your in "safe mode" blah blah. If you get to that point, you can prolly fix it.  If you system doesn't beep, then start tappin' F8 when you see that gateway logo.  I worked tech for GW for 2 years and I've troubleshot lots of these types of issues.  The format fix they tried to give you is pretty typical because they don't want to waste any time.  Also, I cannot remember if ME still gives the startup menu option... oh well, let us know.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Oreif||01-17-2001||02:48 AM||||Monkeyman, From what you describe in your second post, I'd have to agree it is most likely NOT a virus. Try the "Safe Mode". If worse comes to worse you could probably go back to Win98. When in safe mode go to Control Panel and select "Add/Install programs" There should be an "uninstall WinME"<BR>This will return the P.C. to the previous windows version (Win98).<P><BR>Do you know why they call it WinME?<P>It means "Windows with More Errors"!!!!<P>I know a few people who went to ME and are now back at win98.<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 01-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||Monkeyman||01-17-2001||04:09 AM||||I think I tried that safe mode thing.  When I installed ME, I had to make a ME start up disk.  I used that and followed the directions for safe mode.  The only difference I could tell was that it said "Safe Mode" in the bottom corners and the background pic was a negative image.  Other than that, it all was the same.  I also tried the ME restore option.  I went back (supposedly) to the last time it was backed up (although I don't remember backing anything up).  That was in the middle of December.  That didn't help either.  I fully intend to scrap ME if I can ever get back into Windows at all.  About the only thing I like about it was the graphics in Media Player 7 and I'll bet there's a Win98 program out there somewhere that has some cool graphics for music. <P>Let me try a couple of your suggestions and I'll get back soon.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Monkeyman||01-17-2001||04:24 AM||||I guess the safe mode thing isn't going to work.  I tried that and I still come up with the same thing.  The popup windows I get say the same thing with the exception of the file name.  They say:  "_______caused an exception 03H in module USER32.DLL at 0167:bff45d96."  The 1st window says "statemgr", then "scandskw", then "explorer".  After I click on the close button on the last window, nothing happens.<P>The only other thing that I did on the computer at the same time it crashed was to uncheck some of the boxes in the selective startup menu.  This is something that I've done before with the help of the Gateway crew.  Is it possible that I've unchecked one too many things this time? (I try to be very carefull and not uncheck things that I am unsure about.)  This probably doesn't have anything to do with my problem since I think that safe mode bypasses all of that, but I thought I'd throw it in there.<P>Once I get things running again, I think I'll take some computer classes.  I enjoy computers enough to want to know how to mess with them. ||||reg||1
Z||000016||Cliff Pennock||01-17-2001||04:42 AM||||P4n1c, that's CP/M of course...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I got confused with a program I once wrote for CP/M which was called CP/U (Cliff Pennock Utilities). It was similar to what Norton Utilities was for Dos.<P>MM, you can try to boot from the WinMe CDROM. When you see the Gateway startup screen, press the Delete key and you should be taken to the computer's BIOS - be very very careful here since you can totally mess up your system. Somewhere you should be able to change the "First Boot Device" to CDROM. Perhaps anyone here with a Gateway can tell you the exact steps on how to boot from CDROM.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Monkeyman||01-18-2001||12:36 AM||||Back online with the PC.  Also back to Win98.  I used the system recovery disk (forgot I had it) and deleted and reinstalled Win98.  I lost quite a bit of stuff, but not everything.  I've got a few questions as I get things back in order.  I'll ask a few at a time.<P>1)  I can get online but can't get Outlook Express to come up.  Something about not having the right file?  Any ideas about that?  Is there another email program that may be better to use and would still be compatible with what I'm running?  What if I download a later version of OE such as 5.x (4.0 is stock)??<P>2)  Originally, my PC came with a DVD-ROM.  For some reason, I lost the driver to it.  As far as I can tell, the computer just doesn't recognize it as anything but a generic CD-ROM (maybe not even that).  Since I added a separate CD burner, should I remove the burner and see if the computer recognizes just the DVD drive 1st, then add (re-add?) the burner?  <P>More questions later.  Thanx for the help on these. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000018||Oreif||01-18-2001||10:00 AM||||WinME probably upgraded your outlook express. You can go to Microsoft's website and download the Outlook 5.5. This should fix/correct that for you.<P>You may need to contact the manufacturer of the DVD drive to get the new drivers. Since you have the drive they usually send out the drivers for free. Actually if you look up the manufacturer on the net and they have a website, they may have updated drivers you can download. Even some computer stores will make a copy of the drivers for you. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000019||GT Bastard||01-18-2001||02:03 PM||||No drivers needed for the DVD... However, you need to install the Cinemaster software(if they still use Cinemaster) from the recovery cd.  Just pop it in, and a recovery cd window should appear.  You'll be able to select it from a list.||||reg||1
Z||000020||Monkeyman||01-18-2001||04:40 PM||||Thanx, guys.  I'll try those suggestions in a few minutes and see what happens.  More questions soon.||||reg||1
